; Tutorial_8_Do_Math.inix -- working out sums in the editor, and math that reads aloud.

[about]
Title=8 - Do Math
Intro=One want: an answer to a sum without leaving the document. Control plus Equals works out the expression on the current line or in a selection; mathematics written the LaTeX way becomes real math in a web page that the reader speaks.
Setup=EdSharp is open with a new document.
Homework=Write three sums of your own, one per line, and work each out with Control plus Equals.

[step]

Say=One want: a sum worked out right in the document, without a calculator in another window. EdSharp does arithmetic, and more, on the line you are on.
Key=
[step]

Say=Alt plus Control plus E opens EdSharp, with an empty document.
Key=Alt+Control+E
Hear=EdSharp
Hear=Untitled, edit, multiline, blank
[step]

Say=I type a sum as I would write it in code: 1250 times 12, plus 300.
Key=1250 * 12 + 300
Hear=(each character echoed as it is typed)
[step]

Say=Control plus Equals works it out: Equals, the sign that asks for an answer.
Key=Control+Equals
Hear=15300
[step]

Say=The answer is spoken, and placed in the document after the sum, so the working stays on the page beside its result.
Key=
[step]

Say=A planned misstep: a sum with a comma in a number, as we write money.
Key=1,250 * 12
Hear=(each character echoed as it is typed)
[step]

Say=Control plus Equals.
Key=Control+Equals
Hear=15000
[step]

Say=Not the answer I meant. The expression is JScript, and in code a comma separates two expressions, of which only the last counts. Numbers are written without commas.
Key=
[step]

Say=Control plus Z undoes the result, and I fix the number.
Key=Control+Z
Hear=Undo
[step]

Say=Since the expression is JScript, more than arithmetic works. A power: 2 to the 10th.
Key=Math.pow(2, 10)
Hear=(each character echoed as it is typed)
[step]

Say=Control plus Equals.
Key=Control+Equals
Hear=1024
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=A square root, the same way.
Key=Math.sqrt(144)
Hear=(each character echoed as it is typed)
[step]

Say=Control plus Equals.
Key=Control+Equals
Hear=12
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=Rounding to cents: multiply by 100, round, and divide by 100.
Key=Math.round(19.987 * 100) / 100
Hear=(each character echoed as it is typed)
[step]

Say=Control plus Equals.
Key=Control+Equals
Hear=19.99
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=A selection works too. Select several lines of sums, and Control plus Equals works out each one, placing each answer beside its own line.
Key=Control+Equals
Hear=3 lines worked out
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=For more than a line's worth, Control plus Shift plus G opens a more interactive environment, such as an interactive console, where each answer leads to the next question.
Key=Control+Shift+G
Hear=Interactive console
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=Now the second half of math: mathematics to be read by others. Written the LaTeX way, between dollar signs, in a Markdown document, it becomes real math in a web page.
Key=
[step]

Say=I save a new document as a Markdown file, math dot m d, so EdSharp treats it as Markdown.
Key=Control+Shift+S
Hear=Save As dialog
Hear=File name: edit combo
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=Then I write a sentence with a formula in it: the area of a circle is, dollar, backslash pi r caret 2, dollar.
Key=The area of a circle is $\pi r^2$.
Hear=(each character echoed as it is typed)
[step]

Say=Control plus H, HTML Format, converts the document to a web page in a new window, and Pandoc turns the formula into MathML, the standard for math on the web.
Key=Control+H
Hear=Untitled, edit, multiline
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=Saved as a dot h t m file, F5 opens it in the browser, where the screen reader reads the formula as math, not as a row of symbols.
Key=F5
Hear=The area of a circle is pi r squared.
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=And in the browser, the formula can be explored a piece at a time, as a reader of math expects: the reader's own math mode takes you into it.
Key=
[step]

Say=Nothing needs installing for this. Browsers draw MathML themselves, and current screen readers read it, so a page made this way works for every reader.
Key=
[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I say what I want; the reader says the key. Work out a sum.
Key=
Hear=Control plus Equals
[step]

Say=Undo an answer you did not want.
Key=
Hear=Control plus Z
[step]

Say=Open the more interactive environment.
Key=
Hear=Control plus Shift plus G
[step]

Say=Make a web page with real math from Markdown.
Key=
Hear=Control plus H
